{
  "title": "Benché {csi:piovesse}, {im:eravamo} così felici che {pp:siamo usciti}",
  "translation": "Although it was raining, we were so happy that we went out",
  "topics": "Clauses of cause, purpose, concession and result, connecting words",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Which conjunctions take which mood?"
      },
      "A simple logic helps: what's real and stated as a fact, such as a cause or a result, takes the indicative. What is conceded, intended or only conditional takes the *congiuntivo*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Meaning",
            "Conjunctions",
            "Mood",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Cause",
              "perché, poiché, siccome, dato che",
              "indicative",
              "Poiché {im:pioveva}, {pp:siamo rimasti}."
            ],
            [
              "Result",
              "così… che, tanto… che, quindi",
              "indicative",
              "{im:Era} così stanco che {ip:è crollato}."
            ],
            [
              "Purpose",
              "affinché, perché",
              "congiuntivo",
              "Te lo {ip:dico} affinché tu lo {csp:sappia}."
            ],
            [
              "Concession",
              "benché, sebbene, nonostante",
              "congiuntivo",
              "Benché {csi:piovesse}, {pp:siamo usciti}."
            ],
            [
              "Condition",
              "purché, a patto che, a meno che",
              "congiuntivo",
              "{ip:Vengo}, purché tu {csp:guidi}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Siccome* goes at the start of the sentence: *Siccome {im:pioveva}, {pp:siamo rimasti}.* *Anche se* takes the indicative, so it is the everyday alternative to *benché*.",
      {
        "h": "Same subject? Use the infinitive"
      },
      "Purpose is usually expressed with *per* + infinitive when both parts have the same subject: *{ip:Studio} per imparare*, but *Ti {ip:spiego} la regola affinché tu la {csp:capisca}*. Concession can be expressed with *pur* + gerund: *Pur essendo stanco, {pp:è uscito}*.",
      {
        "h": "Nonostante: two uses"
      },
      "As a conjunction, it takes the *congiuntivo*: *Nonostante {csi:piovesse}, {pp:siamo usciti}*. As a preposition, it takes a noun: *Nonostante la pioggia, {pp:siamo usciti}*.",
      {
        "h": "Linking words between sentences"
      },
      "These words connect ideas and organise a text. They do not change the verb.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "Function",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "tuttavia",
              "however",
              "{im:Era} tardi; tuttavia {pp:è uscito}."
            ],
            [
              "inoltre",
              "moreover",
              "{ip:È} economico. Inoltre, {ip:è} comodo."
            ],
            [
              "dunque",
              "therefore",
              "{ip:Penso}, dunque {ip:sono}."
            ],
            [
              "perciò",
              "for this reason",
              "{im:Era} malato, perciò {pp:è rimasto} a casa."
            ],
            [
              "infatti",
              "indeed",
              "Non {ip:sta} bene: infatti non {pp:è venuto}."
            ],
            [
              "invece",
              "instead, on the contrary",
              "{im:Pensavo} di restare; invece {pp:sono partito}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Be careful: *infatti* confirms what was just said, and it never means *in fact* as a correction."
    ]
  },
